WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.700
Your mission, should you choose to accept it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.716
Take a deep dive into the&nbsp;
"Mission: Impossible" franchise.

00:00:06.161 --> 00:00:12.000
The "Mission: Impossible" television&nbsp;
series ran on CBS from 1966 to 1973.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.800
Its opening titles became iconic, with&nbsp;
the imagery of a fuse being lit while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.352
classic theme kicked into high gear. Every&nbsp;
week, team leader Jim Phelps would be told:

00:00:21.352 --> 00:00:23.856
"Your mission, should you decide to accept it..."

00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:26.820
…before getting his assignment.&nbsp;
Every assignment ended with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.960
"This tape will self-destruct in five seconds,"&nbsp;
and the cassette would go up in smoke.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.640
These days, audiences associate Tom Cruise&nbsp;
with "Mission: Impossible" and fans of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.360
classic show will have fond memories of&nbsp;
Peter Graves' Jim Phelps. But there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.080
a famous face that many people may not&nbsp;
realize was behind the original series:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.700
the legendary Lucille Ball. "Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible" was produced by Desilu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:53.940
the production company founded by Ball and her&nbsp;
then-husband and co-star Desi Arnaz. In 1966,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:00.000
Ball and her company set up "Mission" at CBS and&nbsp;
a little sci-fi series named "Star Trek" at NBC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.160
and both shows gave Ball greater clout in the&nbsp;
industry as a producer. "Mission" was a bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.140
hit in the ratings than "Trek," although "Trek"&nbsp;
of course became a big cult phenomenon in reruns.

00:01:10.464 --> 00:01:14.220
The "Mission: Impossible" films not only&nbsp;
have the major star power of Cruise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.060
but they also have had great directors at&nbsp;
the helm, and some of the best writers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.580
the business. The first "Mission" is credited to&nbsp;
David Koepp, Steven Zaillian, and Robert Towne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:28.260
who wrote one of the best films of the '70s,&nbsp;
"Chinatown." Towne came back for the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.520
"Mission," and Hollywood wunderkind J.J.&nbsp;
Abrams came aboard to write and direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.080
the third film. Brad Bird, known for helming&nbsp;
animated classics like "The Iron Giant," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.760
"The Incredibles," made his live-action directing&nbsp;
debut with "Mission Impossible: Ghost Protocol."

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.540
Since then, Cruise has had a steady&nbsp;
partnership with Christopher McQuarrie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.340
who wrote one of the best crime thrillers&nbsp;
of the '90s, "The Usual Suspects." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.660
McQuarrie had his first brush with "Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible" when he joined "Ghost Protocol" for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.040
some uncredited rewrites. His involvement&nbsp;
didn't stop there and he has been in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.580
director's chair for all of the "Mission"&nbsp;
films since "Rogue Nation." In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.600
McQuarrie co-wrote the script for "Top&nbsp;
Gun: Maverick," which became one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.120
biggest box-office smashes of 2022, and he's&nbsp;
also done uncredited script doctor work.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:17.220
McQuarrie also isn't against flying by the seat of&nbsp;
his pants, since he boarded "Mission: Impossible:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.900
Fallout" before a script had even been finished.&nbsp;
According to The Hollywood Reporter, the shoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:28.080
began with 33 pages of script, and McQuarrie and&nbsp;
Cruise improvised from there. As McQuarrie said,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.560
"The finished screenplay actually&nbsp;
confines and limits. [...] All I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.440
really need to know is where is&nbsp;
the location and what assets need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.212
to be there on that day including&nbsp;
vehicles, props, sets and actors."

00:02:39.404 --> 00:02:41.040
"That's all we're gonna say about that."

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.720
Much of the cast of the original "Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible" TV series were around when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.040
first Tom Cruise movie went into production,&nbsp;
but none of them appeared in the film. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.860
it turns out, they weren't happy&nbsp;
with how the story was updated.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.320
As Martin Landau said to MTV News:

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.980
"When they were working on an early&nbsp;
incarnation of the first one — not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.820
the script they ultimately did — they&nbsp;
wanted the entire team to be destroyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:08.040
done away with one at a time, and I was against&nbsp;
that. It was basically an action-adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.420
movie and not 'Mission.' 'Mission' was a&nbsp;
mind game. The ideal mission was getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.260
in and out without anyone ever knowing we&nbsp;
were there. So the whole texture changed."

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.360
Peter Graves wasn't happy with the movie, either,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:24.000
especially considering his character Jim Phelps&nbsp;
was turned into a villain. The actor told CNN:

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.260
"I am sorry that they chose to call him Phelps.&nbsp;
[...] They could have solved that very easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.880
by either having me in a scene from the very&nbsp;
beginning, or reading a telegram from me saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.740
'Hey boys, I'm retired, gone to Hawaii.&nbsp;
Thank you, goodbye, you take over now.'"

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.900
Another original member of the "Mission" team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.994
Greg Morris, reportedly saw the&nbsp;
final product and walked out of it.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:49.320
The '90s saw the beginning of the CGI era,&nbsp;
with computer effects rapidly replacing real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.420
sets and practical photography as filmmakers&nbsp;
gained the ability to create entire worlds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.140
through the power of digital technology.&nbsp;
Of course, virtual sets are commonplace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:03.180
in movies today, but the first "Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible" helped pioneer them in 1996.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.480
Virtual sets took a big step&nbsp;
forward for an action segment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.020
in which Tom Cruise and Jon Voight&nbsp;
fight on top of the Channel Tunnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:14.400
train. As Industrial Light and Magic&nbsp;
VFX supervisor John Knoll explained,

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.840
"We took blue-screen elements of actors and&nbsp;
put them into believable CG backgrounds."

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.800
Cruise is going for more realism with his&nbsp;
stunts these days, and perhaps he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.180
want to film that segment shot on top of a real&nbsp;
train today. Still, this segment in "Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:31.920
Impossible" was a big step forward, opening the&nbsp;
door for practically any mind-blowing action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.678
segment a director can come up with, without the&nbsp;
limitations of imagination, physics, or reality.

00:04:37.678 --> 00:04:39.857
"Oh, boy."

00:04:39.857 --> 00:04:44.040
Even if you don't know the name Alan&nbsp;
Silvestri, you certainly know his music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:48.660
because he wrote the iconic theme to "Back to&nbsp;
the Future." He was also set to be the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:53.160
composer on the first "Mission: Impossible"&nbsp;
movie, but he was replaced by Danny Elfman.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:58.320
Elfman had five weeks to write and record a new&nbsp;
score, but as he explained to Soundtrack Magazine:

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.840
"Anybody coming in second has a huge advantage of&nbsp;
understanding what the director doesn't want — at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.280
the expense of a lot of work already put in.&nbsp;
I knew that they wanted energy; they wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:12.720
something a little more operatic and theatrical,&nbsp;
just seeing the movie without a score."

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.220
For "Mission: Impossible II," Metallica&nbsp;
contributed "I Disappear," and when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.700
song leaked onto Napster, it changed&nbsp;
the landscape of the music industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:26.280
forever. In a landmark case, Metallica sued&nbsp;
Napster over the leak in 2000. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.540
the file-sharing genie was already out&nbsp;
of the bottle and the stage was set for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:35.000
downloads and eventually streaming to become&nbsp;
a huge part of how people listen to music.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.600
Throughout Hollywood history, actors have produced&nbsp;
their own films, started their own production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.520
companies, and in the case of United Artists,&nbsp;
even launched their own studio. Tom Cruise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:49.115
became his own producer on the first "Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible," and this has made him very rich.

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:50.797
"It's mine. It's all mine."

00:05:50.797 --> 00:05:54.780
As Variety reported, Cruise is taking&nbsp;
less money up front for the next "Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:59.760
Impossible" movies, but he's getting a big chunk&nbsp;
of the movie's first-dollar gross. This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.740
he'll be taking home sizable bonuses even before&nbsp;
the studio gets the full return on its investment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.540
Whereas a star normally has to wait for the film's&nbsp;
final back end gross to be tallied before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:14.160
get a piece of the action. Cruise's deal for "Top&nbsp;
Gun: Maverick" means he could make upwards of $100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.475
million, and he's certain to make a truckload&nbsp;
of cash on future "Mission" films as well.

00:06:19.475 --> 00:06:23.400
The "Mission: Impossible" films have always&nbsp;
been known for their stunning action sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.880
and the filmmakers know well enough&nbsp;
by now to put particular focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.240
delivering the goods with each new&nbsp;
installment. Sometimes, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.600
the stories are built around action&nbsp;
scenes, rather than the other way around.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.440
The sole credited screenwriter for&nbsp;
"Mission: Impossible II," Robert Towne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.940
had worked with Cruise previously on&nbsp;
"Days of Thunder" and "The Firm." Towne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:46.320
had worked as a script doctor on a number&nbsp;
of films, including "Bonnie and Clyde" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:51.000
"The Godfather," so he knew how to come in and&nbsp;
deliver great scenes when a movie needed help.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.140
But writing a movie around the action scenes&nbsp;
was something he'd never experienced before.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.600
As Towne told Creative Screenwriting:

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.780
"They said: 'These are the action&nbsp;
scenes. What do you think of coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.980
up with a story to fit them?' I said&nbsp;
it was an insane idea, but I did it."

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:08.700
Towne finally came up with the key to&nbsp;
the story from researching viruses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:12.960
Then the idea dawned on him: What about a&nbsp;
villain who creates a deadly virus so he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.920
can make a fortune selling the antidote?&nbsp;
It's a chilling idea for an action film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:20.528
and it would be even more scary&nbsp;
today in a post-COVID world.

00:07:20.627 --> 00:07:23.160
A lot goes into a movie, and with "Mission:

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:26.460
Impossible — Fallout," McQuarrie&nbsp;
told the world in an Instagram post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:30.600
just what challenges went into making the&nbsp;
movie. The filmmaker posted the following:

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:36.060
"3000 setups, 13 helicopters,&nbsp;
6 pregnancies, 5 hiatuses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:42.480
4 weeks of aerial photography, 3 continents,&nbsp;
2 winters, 1 broken ankle. That's a wrap."

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.280
The broken ankle belonged to Cruise, who was&nbsp;
injured in a stunt. As Cruise explained on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.580
"The Tonight Show," he was supposed to&nbsp;
be running and jumping across buildings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:55.140
when the accident happened. According to&nbsp;
a report by Far Out, Cruise was back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:59.520
action seven weeks after his injury. As to&nbsp;
why he does his own stunts, he explained,

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.980
"It has to do with storytelling... it allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.920
us to put cameras in places that&nbsp;
you're not normally able to do."

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:08.160
Cruise explained how the first&nbsp;
time attempting a stunt is equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.060
parts anxiety and adrenaline. He&nbsp;
mentioned how others tell him he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.374
got a huge grin on his face while&nbsp;
shooting these dangerous sequences.

00:08:15.374 --> 00:08:20.778
"Here's why Tom Cruise gets paid the big bucks."

00:08:20.778 --> 00:08:25.680
As "Mission: Impossible — Dead Reckoning Part&nbsp;
1" was being hyped at CinemaCon 2022 in Vegas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.820
McQuarrie promised the film will feature&nbsp;
the most dangerous stunt the team has ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:34.920
attempted. In order to pull this stunt off,&nbsp;
Cruise needed 500 hours of skydiving training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:39.060
and he had to perform 13,000 bike&nbsp;
jumps to nail the landing perfectly.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:43.140
Beyond the stunts, the "Mission: Impossible"&nbsp;
shoot was also dangerous because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.920
pandemic. Cruise, in particular, made&nbsp;
headlines for some heated exchanges with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.300
crewmembers who failed to follow a strict&nbsp;
masking mandate. Cruise and McQuarrie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:55.440
also both reportedly caught the virus,&nbsp;
while McQuarrie had to be hospitalized.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.340
Apparently, Cruise has had the urge for&nbsp;
dangerous stunts since he was a kid. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:03.120
revealed at the Cannes Film Festival that he&nbsp;
jumped off his roof when he was four years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:08.220
making a parachute out of a sheet. The actor&nbsp;
described how he recognized it was a bad idea as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.980
soon as leapt, describing how he feared dying from&nbsp;
the fall or his mother killing him for doing it.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:18.780
Since COVID hit, the movie industry has been on&nbsp;
shaky ground. A number of movies were delayed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:23.220
productions were shut down, and many were scared&nbsp;
that streaming would wipe out the theatergoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:28.140
experience. As the industry attempts to settle&nbsp;
into a new normal, Tom Cruise is hoping he can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:33.120
save theaters with "Top Gun: Maverick" and the&nbsp;
upcoming "Mission: Impossible" movies. The next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:37.620
"Mission" films will be released in two parts,&nbsp;
and Cruise set up "Top Gun" to have a 90-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:41.520
theatrical window, giving it as much time&nbsp;
as possible to make money at the box office.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.960
Cruise initially wanted "Maverick" to&nbsp;
have a 120-day theatrical run before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.900
the movie went to streaming&nbsp;
and other secondary markets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:54.120
but studio negotiations whittled that down to&nbsp;
90. Forbes speculated that Cruise could use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.440
success of "Maverick" as a bargaining chip for&nbsp;
release terms on the next two "Mission" films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.760
which could also play a big hand in saving&nbsp;
the theatrical experience for movie fans.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.560
As Cruise himself wrote on Instagram:

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.780
"36 years after the first film, 'Top Gun:&nbsp;
Maverick' is here. We made it for the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.800
screen. And we made it for you, the fans.&nbsp;
I hope you enjoy the ride this weekend."

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:18.000
At the Cannes Film Festival, Cruise went on&nbsp;
record to say that he wants to make movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.980
for the theater experience and so that people can&nbsp;
go out and watch them. He went on to promise that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:26.580
"Maverick" never would have been released&nbsp;
direct to a streaming platform. He believes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:31.020
that movies should always be experienced in a&nbsp;
theater, as it's something special people can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:35.000
share with the others around them and&nbsp;
partake in the collective excitement.

